WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.038 --> 00:00:03.621
(light music)

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.270
- The first modern humans

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:15.410
that we know have ever
existed lived in Africa,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.770
and they date to somewhere between 230,000

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.360
and 315,000 years ago.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.700
So modern people have been around

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.360
in Africa for a very long time.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.990
And then sometime around 80,000 years ago,

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.760
it looks like there's
this kind of big movement

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:28.930
out of Africa.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.610
But meantime on the continent of Africa,

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.290
a lot was happening too.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.440
And that's when you actually
start to see the first evidence

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.200
that there was this really
widespread mingling and moving.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:40.910
And in terms of what we
find archeologically,

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.670
this is when you get a real explosion

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.050
in the first art, the first clothing,

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.810
the first personal ornaments,

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.490
and most of that happens first in Africa.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.940
And all of that continues to kind of grow

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.620
and develop and change in
the various parts of Africa,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.240
but we don't know as much about it

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.460
as we do other regions
because there just hasn't been

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.290
that much research that's been done.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.360
I first went into the Mount Hora area

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.550
in Northern Malawi in 2016

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.420
because I wanted to see
what the potential would be

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.340
to start a new project in that region.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.160
I knew somebody had been
excavating there in 1950

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.760
and that not a lot had
happened since then.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.300
So I was interested in the possibility

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.340
of actually being able
to retrieve ancient DNA

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.500
from this area.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.250
And I did put that in some proposals,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.550
which my colleagues reviewed,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.290
and some of those reviewed said

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.620
that it wouldn't be possible

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.320
that ancient DNA would not preserve

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.670
in these tropical environments

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.380
or that the site wouldn't
actually be very old.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.700
And it's been rewarding to be able to see

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.240
that both of those things are not true.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.890
The site is actually quite old.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.701
It goes back into the last Ice Age

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:42.420
and some of the ancient DNA

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.890
that we've been able to recover from there

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.221
is some of the oldest in Africa.

00:01:46.221 --> 00:01:47.520
(light music)

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.400
So a rock shelter is an area

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.960
that's kind of like an overhang

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.790
and people will take refuge under there

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:54.030
and they might build fires

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.470
or bring home stuff that
they've hunted or whatever.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.630
And so there's some living
activities that happen there

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.940
but they also will often
choose those same places

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.540
as places of burial.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.230
And what we ended up discovering

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.880
was all of the food remains

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.200
and the day-to-day activities are recorded

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:09.400
in these rock shelters,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.580
but we also found some human remains.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.710
And some of those human
remains ended up being

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.510
some of the oldest that
have been found in the area,

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:19.530
and they are the oldest to
actually preserve ancient DNA

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.970
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.670
So what we did with that,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.590
was we combined it with
a lot other new data

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.300
with other teams that had been working

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.410
on other parts of that part
of Africa, Eastern Africa,

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.130
and we found that when you kind of combine

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:35.210
all of these data together,

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.550
it really starts to tell
a a big picture story

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.670
about what the ancestral
population structure

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.380
looked like long ago.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.440
It's interesting to
think about the timescale

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.473
because the actual
individuals that we found,

00:02:48.473 --> 00:02:52.980
they range in age from
between about 16,000 years ago

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.220
and 14,000 years ago at the
site that we discovered.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.170
But there are also other
individuals in the study,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.990
and they ranged in time all
the way up to 2,000 years ago.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.320
Every one of those
individuals can be thought

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.110
of as the product of
all of their ancestors

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.000
and all of their ancestors' ancestors.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.600
What we've been able to find by doing this

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.510
is that it looks like
people were really doing

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:14.880
a lot of widespread movement.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.120
They're kind of moving
across the landscape,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.000
they're meeting their mating partners,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.500
and then, of course, the
product is a new person.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.110
And that ends up resulting
in all of these people

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.190
who live in lots of different places,

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:31.400
but they have this kind of
widespread genetic signal.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.400
And then this interesting thing happens.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.130
You start to notice that genetically,

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.260
as well as in terms of
the material culture,

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.430
so this stuff that they're leaving behind,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.360
it looks like people are kind
of sticking closer to home.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.170
(light music)

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.230
People were making ostrich egg shell beads

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:50.840
in similar ways across
Eastern and Southern Africa.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.800
And then after about 30,000 years ago,

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.590
there's divergence in the
styles that they make.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:58.380
And the hypothesis is

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.120
that there was actually sort of barrier

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.383
to the movement of people
around 30,000 years ago.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.990
And so that's actually
exactly the same pattern

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.300
that we see genetically,

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.270
where there's evidence
of widespread movement

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:13.810
and actual contact between people.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.990
And then after about
30 or 20,000 years ago,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:19.070
right at the peak of the last Ice Age,

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.060
those territories get smaller

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.140
and people start to
behave much more locally.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.957
So we're actually seeing
it in both the DNA

00:04:25.957 --> 00:04:28.740
and and the archeological materials too.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.620
This has required
expertise from researchers

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.540
from almost 20 different
countries to do this study,

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.210
and the individuals that
we're presenting here,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:37.840
the ancient individuals come

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.920
from recent archeological excavations

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.260
such as the one I did

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.290
but also from individuals
that had been held in museums.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.920
And so some of my colleagues
were making their contributions

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.060
through sampling of those individuals.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.718
And what that means is that
you have to go to museums

00:04:53.718 --> 00:04:56.950
and you have to be able to find what it is

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:57.840
that you're looking for,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.410
and you need to be able to know

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.580
that it is in good condition.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.340
It shows us how we can connect
the archeological record

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.580
to the genetic record,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.310
and that's one of the things we've done

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.470
in this paper that we're
really proud of is,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:11.140
having archeologists co-write it

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.863
with geneticists side by
side through the whole thing.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.640
I think one of the biggest lessons here

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.651
is that we should always
expect to be surprised.

00:05:19.651 --> 00:05:22.234
(light music)

